Dynamic programming is a method for solving complex problems by breaking them down into simpler overlapping subproblems, solving each subproblem only once, and storing their solutions - ideally using a memory-based data structure. This approach is particularly effective when the subproblems have overlapping solutions, allowing for significant optimization in time complexity. Recursion, on the other hand, is a programming technique where a function calls itself to solve smaller instances of the same problem until a base case is reached. When combined, dynamic programming and recursion can be powerful tools for tackling optimization problems.

One classic problem that benefits from dynamic programming and recursion is the **Longest Common Subsequence (LCS)** problem. Given two sequences of characters, the LCS problem asks for the longest sequence that can be derived from both sequences by deleting some characters without changing the order of the remaining characters. A naive recursive solution would involve checking all possible subsequences, leading to an exponential time complexity. However, dynamic programming allows us to store and reuse results of overlapping subproblems, reducing the time complexity to linear, O(n*m), where n and m are the lengths of the two sequences.

* * *

